from _lib import *

CS3 = module("CS3", "Computing 3: Linux, properly - files, permissions, processes, pipes", "Computing",
  "You installed Linux and you live in it. Now you learn what it is made of: every file is blocks on a disk with a name card, every running program is a process with an owner, and the rule that keeps one broken program from reading another's secrets is the same rule that keeps a whole bank safe. This is where a user becomes an operator.",
  "Abid", "A one-line shell script that prints Abid a daily 'system report' of his own laptop (disk left, uptime, biggest folder) - she writes it, he runs it.",
  [
    lesson("CS3-L1", "A file is blocks and a name card: inodes, links and what 'delete' means",
      ["Explain what an inode is (the card that says where a file's blocks are and who owns it)", "Show that two names can point at one file (a hard link) and that rm removes a name, not always the data", "Use ls -li, stat, ln and df to read the real numbers"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "THE PROBLEM: in CS1B-L0 she learned every byte has an address. A disk is the same: millions of numbered blocks. How does the computer find 'README.md' among them? THE MECHANISM: a filesystem keeps an index - each file has an INODE (define it: a card with the file's owner, size, dates and the list of blocks; NOT the name). Folders are just lists of (name -> inode number). Draw it (one small SVG): a folder list on the left, cards in the middle, blocks on the right. HER HANDS: cd ~/school; ls -li (the first number is the inode); stat README.md (read owner, size, blocks); ln README.md twin.md (two names, one inode - ls -li proves it, same number); echo hi >> twin.md then cat README.md (it changed too - same file); rm twin.md then cat README.md (still there: rm removed a NAME; the data goes only when the last name is gone). df -h (how full the disk is; read one line). SECURITY TIE: 'deleted' files are often still on the disk until the blocks are reused - that is why a disk is wiped, not emptied, before it is given away (LX1 encryption makes this moot - say why). Check: 'What does rm actually remove?' and 'Where is the file's name stored?'"),
    lesson("CS3-L2", "Who may touch what: owner, group, others, and the question behind permissions",
      ["Answer the question: why is a program not allowed to read another user's files?", "Read a permission line (-rw-r--r--) and change it with chmod; explain chown", "Explain what root is, what sudo does, and why daily work is never done as root"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "THE QUESTION (the whole module hangs on it): if one program on her laptop is broken by an attacker, why can it not read her e-mail, her vault, or the system's own settings? THE MECHANISM: every file has an owner and a group; the ten letters of ls -l are three sets of rwx (read, write, execute) for owner / group / everyone else; the kernel checks them on EVERY access - a program runs AS a user and gets only that user's rights. Root (define) ignores the checks - which is exactly why nothing daily runs as root and sudo asks for her password for one command. HER HANDS: in ~/school, ls -l; touch secret.txt; chmod 600 secret.txt (only she can read/write); ls -l shows -rw-------; cat it works; then: sudo -u nobody cat secret.txt -> permission denied - another user cannot read it, and that 'user' could be a broken program. chmod 644 and try again. mkdir private; chmod 700 private. ls -l /etc/shadow (where password hashes live: only root) - the same rule protecting the whole system. Boundary Question: 'If you ran a game as root and the game had a bug, what could the bug reach?' Check: read -rwxr-x--- aloud, who can do what."),
    lesson("CS3-L3", "Processes: every running program is a numbered thing with an owner",
      ["Use ps, top/htop and pgrep to see processes, their owners and what they cost", "Explain PID, parent and child, and what a signal is; stop a process with kill", "Explain why a process cannot read another process's memory (back to CS1B-L0)"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "THE PROBLEM: in CS1B-L0 she saw Task Manager. On Linux the same truth is in text, and text can be searched and scripted. THE MECHANISM: a process is a program that has been started: it has a PID (number), a parent (who started it), an owner (whose rights it has - L2), and its own memory the kernel keeps separate from every other process (the address rule from L0: a program may only read its own bytes). HER HANDS: ps aux | head (read one line: USER PID %CPU %MEM COMMAND); ps aux | grep firefox; pgrep firefox; htop (install with apt if missing - a button is not needed, say the command and explain apt is the package shop) - read the bars, press F10 to leave; sleep 300 & (a process in the background - note the PID); kill <pid> (a SIGNAL: a polite 'please stop'); sleep 300 & then kill -9 (the impolite one - say when each is right). Open two terminals: pstree shows the family tree. Boundary Question: 'If a process could read another's memory, what would an attacker's first target be?' (the password manager). Check: PID, parent, owner, signal - one line each in her words."),
    lesson("CS3-L4", "Pipes: small programs joined into a sentence",
      ["Explain stdin, stdout, stderr and what | does", "Build a four-part pipeline (cat/grep/sort/uniq/wc) on her own files and on a log", "Redirect output with > and >> and errors with 2>"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "THE IDEA that made Unix: each program does one thing and speaks text; a pipe pours one program's output into the next's input. Define stdin (the mouth), stdout (the voice), stderr (the complaint channel). HER HANDS, one per message: cat README.md | wc -l; ls ~/school -R | wc -l (how many things); grep -i python README.md; history | grep cd | wc -l (how many times she typed cd); cat /var/log/syslog 2>/dev/null | tail -20 (if permission denied: sudo journalctl -n 20 - say which log Mint keeps); journalctl | grep -i error | tail; ls > list.txt (voice into a file); ls >> list.txt (append); ls /nothing 2> errors.txt (complaints into a file); the four-part sentence: journalctl | awk '{print $5}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head (which program talks most - she reads the winner). Say: this is how a security person reads a million-line log in ten seconds. Check: 'What does | do, in one sentence?' and 'What is the difference between > and >>?'"),
    code("CS3-CODE1", "Code: Python reads the log the pipe made",
      ["Open a file in Python, loop over its lines, count what matters", "Compare the Python way and the pipe way honestly"],
      "# Count how many lines of a file contain a word.\n# Python can read files just like cat does.\nlines = [\"error: disk full\", \"ok: backup done\", \"error: no network\", \"ok: login\"]\ncount = 0\nfor line in lines:\n    if \"error\" in line:\n        count = count + 1\nprint(\"errors:\", count)\n",
      "The editor runs in the browser, so the list stands in for the file; then she does the real thing in her terminal: nano count.py with open('list.txt') as f: for line in f: ... and runs python3 count.py on the list.txt from L4. Teach open(), the with block (the file closes itself), and that a line ends with a newline character. Then honesty: the pipe did this in one line; Python wins when the rule gets complicated (count errors per hour, say). Boundary Question: what if the file does not exist? (FileNotFoundError - she catches it with try/except and prints a kind message). Save the final program; it goes in ~/school/programs."),
    lesson("CS3-L5", "A script: the pipe written down and run again tomorrow",
      ["Write a bash script with a shebang, make it executable, run it from anywhere", "Use variables, $1 and if in bash; explain PATH", "Explain why a script found on the internet is never run before it is read"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "THE PROBLEM: she will type the same five commands every day (the backup, the report). A script is those commands in a file. HER HANDS: nano report.sh; first line #!/bin/bash (the shebang from LX2-L5: which program runs this); echo \"Report for $(whoami) on $(date)\"; df -h / | tail -1; uptime; du -sh ~/school; save; chmod +x report.sh; ./report.sh. Then: mkdir -p ~/bin; mv report.sh ~/bin/; echo $PATH (the list of folders the shell searches for commands - ~/bin is in Mint's by default after re-login; if not, add it in .bashrc and explain); now 'report.sh' from anywhere. Variables: NAME=$1; echo \"hello $NAME\"; if [ -z \"$1\" ]; then echo 'give me a name'; exit 1; fi. SECURITY: a script is a program with HER rights (L2) - 'curl ... | bash' from a website runs a stranger's commands as her; rule she says: read before run. THE GIFT starts here: the report script for Abid - what would HE want to see? Check: shebang, chmod +x, PATH - each in one line."),
    lesson("CS3-L6", "The wargame: OverTheWire Bandit, levels 0-10, with the lab rule",
      ["Connect to a sanctioned practice server with ssh and explain what ssh is", "Solve the first Bandit levels with the commands from this module, writing one line per level in the logbook", "State the lab rule in her own words"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "WHAT IT IS: OverTheWire is a free practice site that WANTS to be used - each level is a small Linux puzzle and the password for the next level is the prize. It is sanctioned: that is the only kind of target she will ever touch. ssh (define: a secure remote terminal - her keyboard, their computer, encrypted on the way) is the door: the button gives the exact command for level 0. Steps, one per message, and she READS each level's page herself (the page says which commands help - that is the lesson: reading documentation): level 0 -> 1 (cat readme), 1 -> 2 (a file named '-'; why ./- works), 2 (spaces in names), 3 (hidden files, ls -a), 4 (find the human-readable one: file ./*), 5 (find with -size), 6 (find by owner and group), 7 (grep), 8 (sort | uniq -u), 9 (strings), 10 (base64 -d). Each solved level: one logbook line 'Bandit N: what I used and why'. Passwords go in her Bitwarden as 'bandit N' - never in the chat. If she is stuck twice on a level, teach the command, then let her finish it. Stop at 10 today; the rest is practice on her own days.",
      links=[link("OverTheWire Bandit - start here", "https://overthewire.org/wargames/bandit/bandit0.html", "the practice game; each level page tells you which commands help")]),
    lesson("CS3-H1", "Horizon: Linux runs the world - and who checks it",
      ["Say where Linux runs (most servers, every Android phone, the Pi, supercomputers) and why", "Explain open source as the check: anyone can read it, states and companies audit it, and a hidden door would be found", "Give one example of the same mechanism used to protect and to control"],
      HORIZON + "Facts to teach as mechanism: Linux is a kernel started by one student in 1991 and now written by thousands of people from hundreds of companies; because the code is open, governments (several run their own distributions - mention China's Kylin, Russia's Astra, Turkey's Pardus, India's BOSS as examples of the same move) can read every line before trusting it, and so can anyone who finds a bug. Android is Linux underneath. Protect vs control: the same permission system that protects her files lets a company lock a phone so its owner cannot change it. Boundary Question: 'If you could change one line in the kernel that every bank runs, what line would an attacker want, and who would notice?' (code review, signatures, many eyes)."),
    check("CS3-C", "Computing 3 checkpoint", ["inode-and-names", "permissions-rwx", "processes-and-signals", "pipes-and-redirection", "scripts-and-path"], count=10),
    teach("CS3-T", "Explain to a 9-year-old why a broken game on your laptop cannot read your diary, using the words owner, permission and process. Then say what a pipe is with one example you used. Say it out loud first, then write it. 5-7 sentences."),
  ],
  read={"url": "https://www.linux.org/", "title": "Read: linux.org - what Linux is and where it came from"})

CS4 = module("CS4", "Computing 4: the shell as a power tool - text, time and your own automation", "Computing",
  "A security person's day is mostly text: logs, configs, lists, output. The shell is the fastest tool ever built for text, and once you can schedule it, your laptop works while you sleep. This module makes the terminal yours: find anything, change anything, and let the machine repeat it.",
  "the family", "A script that backs up the family's photos from a phone folder to her encrypted SSD every time it is plugged in, with a one-line report - set up for real and shown to Abid on a call.",
  [
    lesson("CS4-L1", "Find anything: find, locate, grep -r, and the shape of a search",
      ["Find files by name, size, age and content across the whole disk", "Explain why grep -r on a folder beats opening files one by one", "Build one search she will reuse (every .py she has written, newest first)"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "THE PROBLEM: 'where is that file?' costs people hours a week. HER HANDS: find ~ -name '*.py' (every Python file she owns); find ~ -name '*.py' -newer ~/school/README.md; find ~/school -size +1M; find / -name '*.log' 2>/dev/null | head (errors to nowhere - L4); grep -rn 'def ' ~/school/programs (every function she has written, with line numbers); grep -rl password ~/school (which files mention it - and why a security person runs exactly this on a server); ls -lt ~/school/programs | head (newest first). Teach the shape: WHERE, WHAT, THEN WHAT (find <where> <test> -exec ...). Boundary Question: 'What does find ~ -name \"*\" -delete do, and why do you never type -delete without running the same find without it first?' Check: three finds from memory."),
    lesson("CS4-L2", "Change anything: sed, awk, cut, tr, and a hundred files in one go",
      ["Replace text in a file with sed and explain -i", "Pull columns with cut and awk; count with awk", "Do one bulk change on a copy of her folder and verify it with grep before and after"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "THE PROBLEM: 40 files say 'school folder' and should say 'school-folder' - by hand is an hour and a mistake. HER HANDS, on a COPY (cp -r ~/school ~/school-copy - the first rule of bulk edits): grep -rl 'school folder' ~/school-copy; sed -i 's/school folder/school-folder/g' ~/school-copy/notes/*.md; grep -rl 'school folder' ~/school-copy (empty = done). cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd (users on her machine); awk -F: '{print $1, $7}' /etc/passwd (who gets which shell); echo 'hello' | tr a-z A-Z; wc -w on her README. Explain the pattern language lightly (s/old/new/g; . and * later). SECURITY: /etc/passwd is readable by all, /etc/shadow is not - she already knows why (CS3-L2). Boundary Question: 'sed -i on the real folder with a typo in the pattern - what happens, and what protects you?' (the copy, git later). Check: she explains -i and -F."),
    code("CS4-CODE1", "Code: the same change in Python, when the rule gets too clever for sed",
      ["Read files in a folder with pathlib, change text, write back", "Say when a shell one-liner is enough and when Python is right"],
      "# Replace a word in many strings - the Python side of sed.\nnotes = [\"my school folder is tidy\", \"the school folder has 3 parts\", \"no change here\"]\nfixed = []\nfor n in notes:\n    fixed.append(n.replace(\"school folder\", \"school-folder\"))\nfor f in fixed:\n    print(f)\n",
      "In the browser the list stands for files; in her terminal she writes fix.py with from pathlib import Path; for p in Path('~/school-copy/notes').expanduser().glob('*.md'): text = p.read_text(); p.write_text(text.replace(...)). Teach pathlib as 'paths as objects'. Then the rule of three (LV1): a one-off is sed; a rule with conditions is Python. Boundary Question: what if a file is not text (a picture)? read_text raises - she filters by suffix first. Save the program."),
    lesson("CS4-L3", "Time: cron, systemd timers, and a machine that works while you sleep",
      ["Explain what cron is and read a crontab line", "Schedule her CS3 report script to run every morning and write to a log", "Check that it ran (the log, journalctl) - trust but verify"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "THE PROBLEM: the backup only protects her if it runs when she forgets. THE MECHANISM: cron is a program that wakes every minute and checks a table (crontab) of 'when: what'. The five fields: minute hour day month weekday - draw them. HER HANDS: crontab -e (pick nano); add: 0 8 * * * ~/bin/report.sh >> ~/report.log 2>&1 (every day at 08:00, voice and complaints into the log); save; crontab -l (list it). To test today: add a line for two minutes from now, wait, cat ~/report.log, then remove the test line. Show systemctl list-timers (Mint's own scheduled jobs: updates, Timeshift) - same idea, newer machinery. SECURITY: a cron line is a way an attacker keeps a foothold after a reboot - so 'crontab -l' and the timers list are things a defender checks on a machine she worries about (DEF1 will do this on the Pi). Check: she reads '30 2 * * 1' aloud."),
    lesson("CS4-L4", "The backup, done right: rsync, the ritual, and proof",
      ["Write the family-photos backup script with rsync and explain each flag", "Make it run when the SSD is plugged in (udev rule or a desktop shortcut - choose and explain)", "Prove a restore: delete one photo on the source, bring it back from the SSD"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "The CS1C-L4 ritual becomes THE GIFT. rsync -av --delete <source> <dest> - say each flag in one line (archive = keep dates and permissions; verbose; --delete mirrors removals, so a mistake on the source also deletes on the mirror - that is why Timeshift snapshots exist too). Source: a folder where the family's phone photos land (Abid decides how they arrive: a USB cable + the file manager is fine; a synced folder later). Script: mount check (if the SSD is not unlocked and mounted at /media/<name>/FAIZA-BACKUP, say so and exit 1), rsync, then 'photos: N files, SSD has X GB free' to a report line. Run it by hand first. Automation: a .desktop launcher on her panel is enough for now (a udev rule is shown as the 'real' way and left for later - honesty about complexity). PROOF: delete one photo on the laptop, rsync back the other way for that one file, open it. Logbook line with the date of the proof. Show Abid on the call."),
    lesson("CS4-L5", "Read the errors: --help, man, the exit code, and the two questions",
      ["Use --help and man without fear; find the one flag she needs", "Read $? and explain exit codes; chain commands with && and ||", "Apply the two questions to any error: what exactly did it say, and what did I assume?"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + "THE SKILL that separates people who can use a computer from people who are used by it: reading what it says. HER HANDS: ls --help | less (q to leave; / to search inside); man rsync then /delete to find the flag; a deliberate error: cp nothing somewhere -> read the message word by word; echo $? (2 = failed; 0 = fine); mkdir x && cd x (the second runs only if the first worked); cd /nope || echo 'no such place'. The TWO QUESTIONS written on her paper: 1) what EXACTLY did it say (copy the line), 2) what did I ASSUME (the file exists, I am in the right folder, I have permission). She solves three planted errors (wrong folder; permission denied; a typo in a flag) using only the two questions - the tutor does not help until she has answered both questions aloud. Check: three exit codes and what && means."),
    lesson("CS4-L6", "cmdchallenge: forty puzzles that make the shell yours",
      ["Solve 15 cmdchallenge puzzles using only this module's tools", "Keep a one-line note per puzzle of the command that worked", "Say which command she reached for most and why"],
      FROM_ZERO + "cmdchallenge.com is a free puzzle site: one task, one command, instant check, in the browser - nothing to install. The button opens it. She does at least 15 in order, each one typed in the site AND understood (the tutor asks 'why did that work?' every third puzzle). Notes go in ~/school/notes/cmdchallenge.md - itself made with the shell (echo >>). When stuck twice: hint the tool, never the answer. This is practice; speed is not the point, reaching for the right tool is.",
      links=[link("cmdchallenge", "https://cmdchallenge.com/", "shell puzzles in the browser; nothing to install")]),
    lesson("CS4-H1", "Horizon: the terminal is where the internet is run from",
      ["Explain why servers have no screens and how operators reach them (ssh, scripts, logs)", "Give one story of a big outage caused by one command, and what defenders changed", "Say what 'infrastructure as code' means in one sentence"],
      HORIZON + "Mechanism: a data centre is thousands of Linux machines with no screens; everything is done the way she now works - ssh, scripts, cron, logs. Stories as mechanism, not gossip: a 2017 cloud outage traced to a mistyped command that removed more servers than intended (name it as 'a large cloud provider'); the lesson defenders took: dry runs, limits on how much one command may remove, two people for dangerous changes. Protect vs control: the same remote access that lets an operator fix a server at 3 am is what an attacker wants most - so keys, not passwords, and logs of every login. Boundary Question: 'You can run one command on a thousand machines at once. What check do you put in front of yourself?'"),
    check("CS4-C", "Computing 4 checkpoint", ["find-and-grep", "sed-awk-cut", "cron-and-timers", "rsync-and-backup", "exit-codes-and-errors"], count=10),
    teach("CS4-T", "Explain to Abid, as if he were new to Linux, how your backup script works and how you proved it, using the words rsync, mirror and restore. Then explain in two sentences what cron is for. Say it out loud first, then write it. 5-7 sentences."),
  ],
  read={"url": "https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/index.html", "title": "Read: the GNU coreutils manual - the small programs you now join into sentences"})

MODULES = [CS3, CS4]
